			<div class="content" id="post-content-2486974">
									

<p>One big thing to consider is that birds seem to rely a <em>lot</em> on the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parallax">parallax</a> effect to judge distance and scale- that's why they bob and tilt their heads so much when examining something when sitting still, and do their best to lock their heads relative to their surroundings when flying, walking, or perched on something that moves. If their eyes are moving, <em>something</em> is moving.</p>
<p>By retreating to the corner of an otherwise empty room, the D-class eliminated all possible parallax that could indicate definite scale. Since the owl seems to be sapient enough to selectively apply its abilities, it's possible that it's sapient enough to intentionally <em>let</em> itself be fooled by optical illusions when it wants to be- and the d-class unintentionally set up a perfect one for a bird.</p>

